;; docs/guides/saffron-guide.md, "Typeclasses": in TYPED TURMERIC a method on an
;; un-narrowed `any` is a DIAGNOSTIC naming the three static ways out -- not a
;; guess, not uncompilable C, and not the runtime dispatch Saffron does.
;;
;; This fixture carried a `#lang saffron` line until S9 (D8) built that
;; dispatch. Keeping it Saffron would have pinned the OLD answer and turned the
;; feature into a failure; keeping the assertion on the Turmeric arm is what it
;; was really about -- the two dialects reach the same resolution state and
;; choose differently, and this is the half that must not drift.
;;
;; The guide quotes both the error and its help line, so both are pinned here.
;; The help text is the load-bearing half: it is what sends a reader to the
;; type-case, and an earlier version of this path had no diagnostic at all when
;; a single instance was in scope.
;;
;; The class is the guide's own `Named`, not a stdlib one: `(hash x)` on an
;; `any` is WELL-TYPED now that `Hash [any]` exists (set-of-element-type-is-not-
;; checked), so the diagnostic is only reachable for a class with no `any`
;; instance -- which is what the guide's example is.
;;
;; The Saffron half is `tests/fixtures/saffron-dyn-typeclass-dispatch`.

(defclass Named [a] (name-of [x] : cstr))
(definstance Named [int] (name-of [x] : cstr "int"))

(defn h [x : any] : cstr (.name-of x))

(defn main [] : int 0)
